INT. PARKED CAR - ABANDONED QUARRY - NIGHT

Rain on the roof. The dashboard glows green. A small digital recorder sits in the cupholder, its red light steady.

DETECTIVE SERAPHINA OKONJO-WYATT (40s, rain-slick trench, eyes that haven't slept in days) leans toward the recorder.

SERAPHINA
This is Detective Okonjo-Wyatt. Eleventh of November, twenty-three-forty. If anyone is listening to this, I'm sorry.

She presses a button. A click. A hiss. Then, on the tape, her own voice plays back from earlier.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
(recorded, flat)
Monday. The body was found at the foot of the quarry stairs. No wallet. No phone. One shoe.

She stops the tape. Rewinds a little. Presses record.

SERAPHINA
The shoe was wrong. Left shoe, size nine, on a right foot. I should have seen it Monday. I saw it Wednesday. Wednesday is too late.

A long breath.

SERAPHINA (CONT'D)
The man in the stairwell wasn't the man we buried. The man we buried was a second man, and the second man had been dead since Friday. The first man is alive. He is walking around in the first man's shoes.

She rubs her eyes. The rain thickens.

SERAPHINA (CONT'D)
His name is Corwin Hale. He's the one who called it in.

She stops. Her face is lit green. She presses play. Her own voice again.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
(recorded)
Corwin Hale found the body at dawn. He said he was jogging. He was in loafers.

She stops the tape.

SERAPHINA
Loafers, Corwin. I wrote it down. You saw me write it down.

She swallows. Looks through the windscreen. Beyond the glass, in the rain, a pair of headlights is turning in at the quarry gate, slowly, very slowly.

SERAPHINA (CONT'D)
(lower)
If this is the last thing I record, I want it noted that I was right.

The headlights grow. She does not look at them. She reaches out and presses the recorder once more.

SERAPHINA (CONT'D)
(into recorder)
Case file is in the glove box. The key is under the seat. Tell Marguerite I did love her.

The light floods the car. Her shadow lunges across the dashboard.

CUT TO BLACK.
